"""Revise article prose only; leave all saved measurements and estimates unchanged."""
from pathlib import Path

HERE = Path(__file__).resolve().parents[1]
path = HERE / 'manuscript.md'
text = path.read_text(encoding='utf-8')
title = 'Incremental value of power and blade-pitch information for wind-turbine shortfall decisions'
text = text.replace('Power measurements and pitch reconstruction in wind-power shortfall decisions', title)

intro = r'''# Introduction

A wind-power forecast can remain unchanged while new turbine measurements arrive. A model acting on that forecast must then decide how much of a possible production shortfall to cover. Recent power output provides a direct update on production, while blade pitch describes the turbine's operating response. The relevant choice is what the additional measurement changes in the action: an input may reduce average loss, change the shortfall left uncovered, or contribute little once other measurements are available. This makes the information already supplied to the decision model central to evaluating an additional channel.

Recent power is an established source of predictive information. Present-output quantile models [@pritchard2011], dynamic power-curve models with latent dynamics [@iversen2017], and adaptive or missing-data-tolerant approaches [@wen2024; @meng2026; @stratigakos2026] provide several ways to exploit it. Operating-state information is also relevant to uncertainty: operation-based modelling has used turbine measurements including blade angle [@kandemir2026], while regime-dependent intervals and state-adaptive recalibration account for changes in forecast uncertainty [@wang2026; @kometsi2026]. These lines of work motivate a conditional comparison. A benefit from pitch relative to a model without recent power need not describe its contribution after direct power histories have been retained. For example, the operation-based predictors in Kandemir and colleagues' study include blade angle but not autoregressive active power.

Pitch reconstruction raises a related question. Measured pitch introduces an observation, whereas a pitch estimate computed from available histories transforms those inputs using historical pitch supervision. Lower reconstruction error establishes that the histories predict pitch; it does not establish how much this transformation adds to a decision model receiving the same histories. The distinction between forecast quality and user value is well established [@bessa2011], including in the use of probabilistic forecasts for reserve requirements [@zhao2021; @sun2022]. Comparisons of post-processing stages [@phipps2022] and uncertainty sources [@dantas2026] further show why an additional modelling step must be assessed where it enters the forecast process. Here the comparison is made downstream of a common forecast, using both a fixed asymmetric loss and the procurement and uncovered-shortfall quantities that compose it.

We compare power and pitch information in a retrospective shortfall task using ten-minute SCADA records from 134 wind turbines. One analysis contrasts current power and pitch updates after fresh or older histories. A second retains direct power histories and tests features reconstructed from them, with measured pitch and alternative learned scores as comparators. Keeping the forecast and future target common within each analysis makes the added input's decision consequences directly comparable. We then examine whether small total-cost differences also imply similar procurement and uncovered shortfall. The study thereby connects the choice of an additional measurement or reconstructed feature to the action it supports, rather than inferring that choice from reconstruction error alone.

'''

results = r'''# Results

## Updating power improved cost more than updating pitch

We first compared the two measurement updates after holding the upstream forecast fixed. That forecast uses wind and power histories ending one hour before the decision and predicts a single ten-minute power target one hour after it. Each decision model chooses a nonnegative action against the positive forecast shortfall. We evaluate procurement $R$, uncovered shortfall $U$, their weighted cost $C=R+10U$, and the frequency $V$ with which shortfall exceeds the action. Figure 1 locates the additional measurements within this task.

Fresh histories contain the latest 36 ten-minute observations of wind, active power and reactive power. Older histories omit the latest six slots. After older histories, the power update adds current power and two specified power differences; the pitch update adds the current mean of the three blade angles. Both updates together, and pitch after fresh histories, complete the six input configurations. All six are compared on the same 671,538 turbine-interval cells per fit.

![**Figure 1. Measurement updates after a common forecast.** **a**, A shortfall action at $t$ concerns the single ten-minute target at $t+6$. The common forecast uses upstream wind and power histories ending at $t-6$; one step is ten minutes. Current power or pitch can update the action without changing that forecast. **b**, Six input configurations distinguish history freshness from the addition of current power and pitch. Fresh histories already include current power. The power update also includes forecast-minus-current-power and current-minus-last-old-power differences. These are comparisons of specified input packages.](figures/fig1_information_and_timing.pdf){width=100%}

Updating power gave the larger cost improvement. With histogram gradient boosting (HGB), it reduced cost after older histories by 13.17% [8.09%, 18.24%], compared with 3.51% [0.49%, 6.52%] for the pitch update. The direct paired contrast favoured power: its relative cost difference was −10.01% [−13.74%, −6.28%], using the pitch-update cost as denominator (Fig. 2a). This ordering persisted with a multilayer perceptron (MLP), whose direct cost difference was −14.81% [−19.59%, −10.03%]. Applying a shared historical offset level also preserved it: the differences were −10.00% [−13.96%, −6.04%] for HGB and −14.67% [−19.17%, −10.16%] for MLP (Fig. 2c,d).

The size of the pitch increment depended on the reference inputs. After older histories, pitch improved HGB cost, though less than power did. After fresh histories, its cost-reduction estimate was −0.13% [−0.43%, 0.16%]; the corresponding MLP estimate was less precise. Adding pitch after a power refresh likewise produced little change in mean cost. Thus, pitch's improvement over older histories did not carry over as a similarly sized increment once recent power was present.

![**Figure 2. Power and pitch updates have different cost effects.** HGB (**a,c**) and MLP (**b,d**) are shown with individually selected offsets (**a,b**) and a shared offset level (**c,d**). Negative values indicate lower cost for the added-input model, or for power relative to pitch in the fourth row. Points are five-fit mean contrasts and bars are approximate simultaneous 95% intervals from shared three-day temporal blocks. Comparisons share 671,538 cells per fit over 35 days. A dagger indicates that at least one endpoint did not meet the event-rate requirement under the corresponding calibration procedure. The interval families and qualification rules are specified in Methods and Supplementary Methods.](figures/fig2_measurement_cost.pdf){width=100%}

Cost and event frequency gave different assessments of these models. Several individually calibrated models exceeded the requirement that each fit's event-rate upper bound be at most 10%. Shared calibration brought more models within that requirement, including both endpoints of the MLP power-versus-pitch comparison, although the HGB pitch-update endpoint remained outside it. The favourable power-versus-pitch cost contrast therefore describes a ranking of the input packages; event qualification must also be considered when choosing between them.

## Reconstructed pitch added little beyond the histories used to predict it

The measurement comparison leaves open whether pitch can instead be recovered from the information already available. To examine that question, the reconstruction analysis begins with wind histories and the common forecast, then adds direct active- and reactive-power histories. Those direct histories reduced cost by 7.69%, an absolute paired difference of −2.734 kWh-equivalent per turbine-interval [−4.853, −0.837]. All five fits and the one-, three- and seven-day block analyses favoured the addition (Fig. 3a). The lower cost nevertheless came with a change in risk: observed event frequency increased from 10.15% to 11.57%.

These power histories also predicted pitch. The pitch-supervised estimator had lower reconstruction error in every fit than either a base-input estimator or a model trained on shuffled pitch targets (Fig. 3c). We next added its pitch estimate to the decision model that already received the direct histories. Cost decreased by 0.21%, an absolute difference of −0.068 [−0.171, −0.004], much smaller than the improvement from adding the histories themselves. Only three of five fits favoured this addition, and its one-day interval crossed zero.

The alternative scores further limit what this small increment says about pitch reconstruction. The pitch-supervised score did not separate from either score control in the primary cost analysis (Fig. 3b). A learned feature may therefore help the fitted decision model, but the comparison does not distinguish the contribution of pitch supervision from that of the alternative representations. Measured pitch also gave a favourable primary difference, −0.132 [−0.272, −0.016], with a one-day interval crossing zero. These additions are each compared with the direct-history model; their reported intervals do not provide a direct measured-versus-recovered comparison.

Reconstruction also failed to replace the histories in a smaller decision head. Retaining the base inputs and one recovered score, while removing the direct-history columns from that head, increased cost by 1.720 [0.361, 3.591]. The score's computation still required those histories. Predicting pitch from the histories and preserving their decision performance in a single feature were therefore different outcomes (Supplementary Table S4 and Supplementary Fig. S1).

![**Figure 3. Pitch reconstruction and incremental decision value.** **a**, Cost effects of adding direct power histories and replacing them with a compressed decision head. **b**, Incremental cost effects of recovered pitch, measured pitch and the two score controls, on a smaller labelled scale. Points and bars are paired mean differences and approximate simultaneous 95% intervals from three-day blocks, conditional on five fitted instances and 35 shared weather days. The cost population contains 672,939 future-power-valid cells per fit. **c**, Pitch mean squared errors for the five fits, shown individually on a logarithmic scale. Reconstruction errors use current-pitch-valid cells; their mask differs from the cost population. The compressed head has 40 base inputs and one score; generating the score still uses the direct histories.](figures/fig3_reconstruction_and_controls.pdf){width=100%}

## Measured pitch changed the balance between procurement and shortfall

Small changes in total cost did not imply similar actions. In the shared-calibration HGB comparison, adding measured pitch after a power refresh increased procurement by 0.96% and reduced mean uncovered shortfall by 9.57%. Total cost increased by only 0.09%, with a relative difference interval of [−0.47%, 0.64%]. Both models met the event requirement. Their procurement and weighted-shortfall changes largely offset in the combined loss (Table 1; Fig. 4).

\input{tables/table1.tex}

This trade-off was less clear with MLP, whose weighted-shortfall interval included zero. Nor did historical procurement matching isolate an advantage from pitch at equal procurement. The matching failed to maintain equal procurement in evaluation: the shared-calibration HGB pitch-added model procured 29.984 kWh per cell, compared with 29.501 for the historically matched power-only model. The latter's observed event frequency was 20.07%. Matching average procurement before evaluation did not hold that budget constant in the later period.

![**Figure 4. Procurement and uncovered shortfall can offset in total cost.** Effects of adding pitch after a power refresh are shown for HGB and MLP, with individual offsets (open markers) and a shared offset level (filled markers). **a**, Procurement difference relative to the historical reference cost $C^*$. **b**, Ten times the uncovered-shortfall difference relative to $C^*$. **c**, Relative cost difference using the power-update model's cost as denominator. Bars are approximate simultaneous 95% intervals from the shared three-day block analysis. Different denominators are labelled explicitly: panel **b** is a weighted shortfall contribution, not the relative percentage change in uncovered shortfall.](figures/fig4_procurement_shortfall.pdf){width=100%}

Removing pitch also had consequences that its small total-cost increment did not capture. In fitted HGB instances, replacing the power-plus-pitch model with the power-update model increased uncovered-shortfall severity beyond the specified allowance. Other comparisons remained unresolved at the cost margins. The joint cost, event and shortfall-severity criteria were not met by either estimator under either calibration procedure (Supplementary Tables S7–S8). The observed cost ranking consequently supports the value of a power update without establishing that pitch can be removed while preserving all three requirements.

'''

discussion = r'''# Discussion

Direct power information provided the clearest cost improvements in this wind-turbine shortfall task, but its strength as a predictor did not settle the choice of additional inputs. A power update outperformed a pitch update after older histories. Once direct histories were retained, reconstructing pitch produced a much smaller cost increment, while measured pitch could change procurement and uncovered shortfall with little net change in cost. Read together, these findings show why the value of an operating-state variable depends on both the reference information and the consequences used to assess it.

The first dependence connects the present comparisons to existing wind-power forecasting research. Present-output quantiles and dynamic power curves already establish the relevance of direct power [@pritchard2011; @iversen2017]. Operation-based forecasting and state-dependent calibration address how operating context enters a predictive distribution [@kandemir2026; @kometsi2026]. Our comparisons ask what remains to be gained from measured or recovered pitch when direct power is explicitly retained downstream of a common forecast. They put the two sources of information in the same decision task without treating either a physically meaningful channel or a reconstructed state as beneficial by construction.

For reconstruction, the critical reference was the direct input used to generate the feature. Lower pitch error showed that the histories contained information about pitch. The decision model, however, already had those histories. Adding an estimate could change how a restricted learner used them, or draw on useful historical supervision, without adding a new observation. The smaller decision increment and unresolved score controls are consistent with these possibilities. The unsuccessful compressed head further distinguishes a useful representation from one that preserves the performance of its inputs. This interpretation concerns the evaluated models and features; it does not establish that pitch information is redundant.

The second dependence concerns what counts as improvement. The separation of forecast quality from user value is longstanding [@bessa2011]. Here it also matters within the decision objective itself: the same weighted cost combines procurement and uncovered shortfall, so opposing changes in those quantities can leave the total nearly unchanged. The measured-pitch HGB comparison gives a concrete instance of that trade-off. It explains why a small cost increment is insufficient grounds for substituting inputs when shortfall severity matters separately. Because procurement matching did not persist and the MLP shortfall effect was less resolved, the comparison does not identify a pitch-specific allocation gain at equal budget.

The scope of this result is a retrospective comparison of specified input packages. The packages include different engineered features, and the measurement and reconstruction analyses share one weather window while using different fitting budgets and validity masks. They answer related questions without providing independent replications or a pooled estimate. The early historical pitch-fitting features have the publication-time exception described in Methods. Temporal-block intervals address weather dependence conditional on the fixed fits, with greater sensitivity for the smaller increments. Applying the findings to net wind-farm shortfall or delivered reserve would additionally require aggregation and delivery constraints absent from this turbine-level task.

The evidence therefore favours assessing additional telemetry against an explicit direct-power reference and reading its shortfall consequences alongside its total loss. In the comparisons here, power supplied the larger cost gains, pitch reconstruction offered a small and less consistent increment, and measured pitch affected a procurement–shortfall trade-off that the cost ranking alone did not resolve.

'''

start = text.index('# Introduction\n')
end = text.index('# Methods\n')
text = text[:start] + intro + results + discussion + text[end:]
abstract = '''  Selecting an additional wind-turbine measurement requires knowing its contribution beyond existing decision inputs. We compare current-power updates, measured blade pitch and reconstructed pitch in a fixed shortfall task using records from 134 turbines over a shared 35-day evaluation period. Power updates gave larger cost improvements than pitch updates after older histories: the direct histogram-boosting contrast favoured power by 10.01% (approximate simultaneous 95% interval, −13.74% to −6.28%). The ordering persisted with a neural estimator and shared calibration. In a complementary analysis, direct power histories improved cost substantially more than adding pitch reconstructed from those histories; the smaller reconstruction increment was sensitive to temporal block length and did not separate from score controls. Measured pitch nevertheless changed the action balance. Under shared histogram-boosting calibration, adding it after a power refresh reduced mean uncovered shortfall by 9.57% while increasing procurement by 0.96%, with little net cost change. Lower cost with power did not establish that pitch could be removed while preserving shortfall protection. These retrospective comparisons distinguish a strong direct-power cost reference from the additional shortfall consequences of pitch, showing why reconstruction error or total cost alone cannot settle the choice of inputs.
'''
a = text.index('abstract: |\n') + len('abstract: |\n')
b = text.index('---\n', a)
text = text[:a] + abstract + text[b:]
path.write_text(text, encoding='utf-8')
si = HERE / 'supplementary.md'
si.write_text(si.read_text(encoding='utf-8').replace('Power measurements and pitch reconstruction in wind-power shortfall decisions',title),encoding='utf-8')
print('Updated prose and matching SI subtitle; Methods and numerical source files unchanged.')
